From: Adriel Santos <adriel@adriel.eu>
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: ath11k: keep sends blocked until recovery sets the rings up

ath11k_core_reconfigure_on_crash() clears the HAL ring table with
ath11k_hal_srng_clear() and then clears ATH11K_FLAG_CRASH_FLUSH before
calling ath11k_core_qmi_firmware_ready(), which sets the CE rings up
again only after its QMI exchanges with the firmware, tens of
milliseconds later. mac80211 keeps calling into the driver during a
recovery, so a WMI command sent in that window passes the CRASH_FLUSH
check in ath11k_ce_send() and reads the cleared ring's NULL tail pointer
in ath11k_hal_srng_access_begin(), with BH disabled: the kernel panics.

On an IPQ5018/QCN6122 AX3000T this reset the board when a user PD was
restarted after a double firmware crash. mac80211 had been sending the
same burst of WMI commands every 6.066 s (failing with -ESHUTDOWN) and
the oops came exactly when the next burst was due, 37 ms after the
recovery's QMI device info exchange. A single recovery has the same
window, only rarely with a sender in it.

Set CRASH_FLUSH again when ath11k_core_qmi_firmware_ready() starts on a
registered device and clear it once the CE and DP rings are set up,
before ath11k_core_start() needs WMI, unless the firmware crashed again
meanwhile (fw_crash_counter moved), which must keep sends failing fast.
ath11k_core_reconfigure_on_crash() still clears the flag first, so also
check in ath11k_ce_send() that the ring is set up before touching it.

--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/core.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/core.c
@@ -2300,6 +2300,7 @@
 
 int ath11k_core_qmi_firmware_ready(struct ath11k_base *ab)
 {
+	u32 crashes = 0;
 	int ret;
 
 	switch (ath11k_crypto_mode) {
@@ -2316,6 +2317,19 @@
 		return -EINVAL;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * A crash recovery gets here with the HAL ring table cleared, and
+	 * mac80211 still sends WMI commands. Keep them failing with -ESHUTDOWN
+	 * until the rings are set up again below: the QMI exchanges before
+	 * that take tens of milliseconds.
+	 */
+	if (test_bit(ATH11K_FLAG_REGISTERED, &ab->dev_flags)) {
+		set_bit(ATH11K_FLAG_CRASH_FLUSH, &ab->dev_flags);
+		spin_lock_bh(&ab->base_lock);
+		crashes = ab->stats.fw_crash_counter;
+		spin_unlock_bh(&ab->base_lock);
+	}
+
 	ret = ath11k_core_start_firmware(ab, ab->fw_mode);
 	if (ret) {
 		ath11k_err(ab, "failed to start firmware: %d\n", ret);
@@ -2334,6 +2348,12 @@
 		goto err_firmware_stop;
 	}
 
+	/* unless the firmware crashed again meanwhile (QMI server exit) */
+	spin_lock_bh(&ab->base_lock);
+	if (ab->stats.fw_crash_counter == crashes)
+		clear_bit(ATH11K_FLAG_CRASH_FLUSH, &ab->dev_flags);
+	spin_unlock_bh(&ab->base_lock);
+
 	if (ath11k_frame_mode == ATH11K_HW_TXRX_RAW)
 		set_bit(ATH11K_FLAG_RAW_MODE, &ab->dev_flags);
 
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/ce.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/ce.c
@@ -760,6 +760,12 @@
 
 	spin_lock_bh(&srng->lock);
 
+	/* cleared by a crash recovery that has not set the rings up yet */
+	if (unlikely(!srng->initialized || !srng->u.src_ring.tp_addr)) {
+		ret = -ESHUTDOWN;
+		goto err_unlock;
+	}
+
 	ath11k_hal_srng_access_begin(ab, srng);
 
 	if (unlikely(ath11k_hal_srng_src_num_free(ab, srng, false) < 1)) {
